	(text "AVDD (pin 7) and PVDD (pin 15) are REQUIRED even though AUXADC0-5 are unused and\neven if the PLL were unused -- ADAU1466 Rev.C p.33, V-22. Do not drop either rail.\nThe six AUXADC no-connects below make 'drop AVDD' look like a free optimisation. It is not."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 34.29 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "bc6528c6-1390-5d1a-95cf-f000f348a222")
	)
	(text "R1 holds RESET low if its driver is absent or high-Z (Rev.C p.34), backing up the\ninternal pull-down. Fail-safe polarity: unpowered = held in reset."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 45.72 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "91bc5fe3-394d-526a-ae5a-be9a0d514d92")
	)
	(text "Every pin on this sheet has an explicit disposition -- net label or no-connect (F28).\nUnused-pin policy is PER PART, sourced from that part's own datasheet. No board-wide default."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 55.88 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "6902e54d-3867-5861-aa87-aef7d9fea810")
	)
	(text "S3 [HARD] CLOCK TREE -- ONE XO, ONE FANOUT, FOUR POINT-TO-POINT LINKS. NO DAISY CHAIN.\n\n  Y1  24.576 MHz LVCMOS  ->  U50 LMK1C1104  ->  Y0  MCLK_DSP    (U1  pin 21)\n                                                Y1  MCLK_ADC    (U40 pin 16)\n                                                Y2  MCLK_DAC_A  (U51 pin 1)\n                                                Y3  MCLK_DAC_B  (U52 pin 1)\n\nFOUR OUTPUTS, FOUR LOADS, 1:1 -- AND THAT IS A CHANGE. Both AK4458s previously shared one\nMCLK_DAC net, which left the fourth fanout output unused and forked a clock trace into two\nloads. A fork is an impedance discontinuity and a reflection. It is not literally a daisy\nchain, but the buffer exists precisely so that no converter's clock depends on another's\nstub, and there was no reason to spend that. Split into MCLK_DAC_A / MCLK_DAC_B.\n\n24.576 MHz = 512 fs at 48 kHz, which is what lets ONE oscillator serve every converter:\n  AK5574 512fs (V-04)   AK4458 512fs or 768fs at 30-54 kHz (T1-26)   ADAU1466 PLL input\n\nMCLK INPUT REQUIREMENTS, VERIFIED AGAINST THE PART HARDEST TO SATISFY (Rev.C p.29):\n  f_MCLK  2.375 .. 36 MHz          -> 24.576 MHz sits mid-range\n  DUTY -- AND THE BINDING NUMBER IS THE CONVERTER'S, NOT THE DSP's. The ADAU1466 asks\n  only 25..75 % (p.29). THE AK5574 ASKS 45..55 % (dCLK, S11 Switching Characteristics\n  p.19, figure read) -- 2.5x tighter, and a real specification some oscillators miss\n  where 25..75 % is cleared by every LVCMOS part ever made. CITE THE CONVERTER.\n  AND THE BUDGET DOES NOT CLOSE BY ITSELF: the LMK1C1104 specifies output duty 45..55 %\n  but ONLY for a 50 % input -- its duty transfer for a non-50 % input is not\n  characterised at all. XO 45..55 -> buffer 45..55 -> converter needs 45..55 puts every\n  element at the same limit with NOTHING allocated to the buffer. SO THE XO IS SPECIFIED\n  TIGHTER THAN ITS LOAD: 48..52 %, leaving the buffer 3 % each side. Many parts hold\n  +/-2 %; it costs nothing and turns an unallocated term into a stated one.\n  ALL FOUR LOADS SEE THE BUFFER, not the XO, so there is ONE duty requirement here and\n  it is the converters' 45..55 % measured at the buffer output.\n  Pin 21 accepts a clock DIRECTLY; XTALOUT (22) is a no-connect because we drive the pin\n  rather than hang a crystal on it.\n\n+3V3_CLK IS ITS OWN RAIL AND THAT IS F33's REASON, NOT A CONVENIENCE: the clock tree is the\none place where supply noise becomes PHASE noise, and phase noise at the converters is not\nremovable downstream by any filter, digital or analog."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 66.04 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "9bebffc6-7f5f-55f0-ac74-9819a01d10c9")
	)
	(text "TWO ENABLE PINS, BOTH DRAWN, BOTH FAIL-SAFE TOWARD KEEPING THE CLOCK RUNNING.\n\nR55 10k PULLS 1G UP TO +3V3_CLK, AND IT IS A FITTED PART, NOT A DISPOSITION (F21).\nT1-68: the LMK1C1104's 1G carries an INTERNAL 300 kOhm PULL-DOWN and 'switches the outputs\ninto a low state when 1G is low'. So a floating 1G does not degrade the clock tree --\nIT TURNS THE WHOLE THING OFF, and no MCLK reaches any converter on the board. A DNP here\nis a dead board, which is why the resistor is drawn rather than noted. 10k against the\ninternal 300k holds 1G at 0.968 x VDD.\n\n1G IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* ON A GPIO -- see CLKNOTE3. Gating this pin from the MCU is the\npower-saving idea that breaks the sequencing precondition, and the way to make sure it\ngets considered rather than assumed is to make it require a schematic edit.\n\nR54 1k HOLDS THE XO's PIN 1 HIGH. The part is not pinned (F56) and pin 1 is OE, ST or NC\ndepending on the variant that arrives. High is the enabled state for OE and for ST, and it\nis harmless on an NC variant -- so ONE resistor is correct for every candidate, and the\nposition exists so the level can be moved without cutting a track.\n\nSOURCE TERMINATION AT EVERY OUTPUT -- R50..R53, FITTED, CURRENTLY 0R.\n  LMK1C1104 R_OUT = 50 Ohm typ at VDD = 3.3 V (SNAS791D, table read). Source termination\n  wants   R_series = Z0 - R_OUT\n  so THE VALUE CANNOT BE FINALISED HERE: Z0 depends on the stackup and the stackup is\n  UNSET (Block D/E). Per S0 the step that depends on it does not run -- but the step that\n  does not depend on it does, so the POSITION is fitted at 0R and the change is a value\n  rather than a respin. HANDED TO BLOCK E EXPLICITLY:\n    * state Z0 for the four MCLK traces, then set R50-R53 = Z0 - 50 Ohm\n    * LENGTH MATCHING IS NOT REQUIRED. These are four independent point-to-point MCLK\n      links, not a bus; nothing compares their arrival times. What matters is that each\n      is short, direct, and over an unbroken reference plane.\n      BUT BOUND THE ABSOLUTE LENGTH, WHICH IS THE CONSTRAINT THAT DOES BITE: source\n      termination assumes an unterminated high-impedance far end, and at 0.35 ns edges\n      the transmission-line regime starts near 50-60 mm. KEEP EACH LINK UNDER ~50 mm.\n      Why matching is pointless: the buffer's own output-output skew is 25 ps typ /\n      50 ps max, MORE THAN ~8 mm OF TRACE -- matching tighter than the part's own skew\n      does not even reach the dominant term. AK5574 S12 also states outright that MCLK\n      must be synchronised with BICK/LRCK 'but the phase is not important'.\n    * the fanout's own output-output skew is 25 ps typ / 50 ps max -- orders below\n      anything a 48 kHz system can resolve, so skew is not the budget item. JITTER is.\nJITTER: THE SPEC IS UNQUOTABLE AT OUR OPERATING POINT, SO THE BUDGET IS WRITTEN INSTEAD\nAND THE SPEC STOPS MATTERING -- the same move that closed V-26.\n  Jitter-limited SNR:  SNR = -20 log10( 2.pi . f_sig . t_j )\n  At f_sig = 20 kHz:   121 dB needs t_j <= 7.09 ps RMS       112 dB needs 20 ps\n  LMK1C1104 additive jitter 8 fs typ / 20 fs max -- 355x BELOW the 121 dB budget. Its\n  test conditions do not match us (f_IN 156.25 MHz; slew 2 V/ns against a 0.1 V/ns\n  minimum; integration 12 kHz-20 MHz, which exceeds half our carrier). Degrade it 10x\n  for slew and it is still 35x below, and it adds in QUADRATURE with the oscillator.\n  CONSEQUENCE WORTH MORE THAN THE NUMBER: a commodity 24.576 MHz XO at ~1 ps clears\n  7.09 ps by 7x. JITTER IS NOT THIS OSCILLATOR'S BINDING CRITERION -- duty and\n  frequency stability over -20..+70 degC are. S3 [HARD] does say LOW-JITTER XO, so\n  relaxing that is the OPERATOR'S call, not a design economy to be taken quietly.\n  'DROP THE LMK AND FAN OUT FROM CLKOUT' IS NOT AVAILABLE -- AND HERE IS THE\n  ARITHMETIC, NOT JUST THE VERDICT. A verdict invites challenge; a verdict with its\n  arithmetic attached answers the challenge in advance (adversary 010).\n    ADAU1466 CLKOUT jitter = 12 typ / 106 ps max, CYCLE-TO-CYCLE RMS (Table 6, p.9).\n    Budget = 7.09 ps RMS for 121 dB at 20 kHz.   106 / 7.09 = 15x OVER, ON THE RAW\n    FIGURE, BEFORE any metric conversion is attempted at all.\n    Cycle-to-cycle RMS is NOT aperture jitter and the two DO NOT INTERCONVERT -- but\n    no c2c-to-aperture relationship is favourable enough to recover 15x, let alone\n    the four orders of magnitude against the buffer. THE GAP SURVIVES THE CAVEAT.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 139.7 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "98875fda-a26a-5176-b411-f8c9f25b3fba")
	)
	(text "NO SPREAD SPECTRUM ON THIS SHEET. EVER. S3 [HARD], RESTATED IN COPPER BECAUSE THIS IS\nWHERE THE FIX WOULD BE PROPOSED.\nThe bucks on 01_power DO use spread spectrum and that is correct and decreed. If an EMI\nproblem appears at 24.576 MHz or a harmonic, the tempting fix is to dither this clock.\nThat converts a narrow spur into MODULATED JITTER at the converters -- broadband noise in\nthe audio band, removable by nothing downstream. The prohibition covers the AUDIO CLOCK\nTREE ONLY; it is not a general EMC posture, and the two must not be conflated in either\ndirection.\n\nRAILS-UP-BEFORE-PDN IS A PROPERTY OF THIS TREE, STATED HERE BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE THE\nPROPOSAL THAT BREAKS IT WOULD ARRIVE.\nADAU1466 Rev.C p.24: 'the crystal oscillator circuit must provide a stable master clock to\nthe XTALIN/MCLK pin by the time the PVDD supply reaches its nominal level.' READ THE\nCONDITION WITH IT: that sentence is written for the CRYSTAL case and we drive the pin\nexternally, so it is the vendor's statement of intent rather than a spec that literally\nbinds our configuration. The same page adds that the pin is gated internally until the\nDVDD POR and the PVDD-to-DVDD level shifter initialise, and that boot needs eight MCLK\ncycles. S7.2 step 5a-pre separately requires MCLK running before PDN release.\n\nSATISFIED BY CONSTRUCTION, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON IT IS NOT A SEQUENCING STEP: the tree\nruns from +3V3_CLK, up with the analog rails, before the MCU boots and long before any PDN\nis released -- and both enables are hard-tied. There is no ordering firmware can get wrong,\nbecause there is no ordering firmware controls.\n\n  THE PROPOSAL THAT BREAKS IT: 'gate the fanout from a GPIO to save power in standby.'\n  It moves MCLK from a rail-timed guarantee to a firmware-timed one, at the pin whose\n  failure mode is SILENCE rather than degradation. If it is ever wanted, S7.2 gains a real\n  step and BRINGUP gains a real test. It is not a resistor change.\n\nVERIFIED AT BRING-UP RATHER THAN TRUSTED: scope MCLK AT THE ADC PIN, not at the buffer,\nand confirm it is running before PDN rises. BRINGUP.md S4 carries the line."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
